1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Forged from high quality steel or chipped from solid rock.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:10,000
The weapon that blasted through shields and cut through armor.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:17,000
The multi-purpose tool that laid down forests and hewed the lumber that built the American dream.

4
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:22,000
The life-saving device always kept close at hand.

5
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:25,000
I'm here today because I had my axe with me.

6
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:29,000
Probe the secrets of this deceptively simple device.

7
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:34,000
Now, the untold story of axes on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:45,980 --> 00:00:47,980
Three, two, one, go!

9
00:00:48,980 --> 00:00:50,980
It's one of our most basic tools

10
00:00:50,981 --> 00:00:53,000
and one of the most powerful.

11
00:00:53,001 --> 00:00:56,000
In the hands of an expert, it can chop through 14 inches

12
00:00:56,001 --> 00:01:02,000
chop through 14 inches of solid wood in a matter of seconds.

13
00:01:02,001 --> 00:01:04,500
And no one wields an axe with more precision, speed,

14
00:01:04,501 --> 00:01:08,000
and cutting power than competitive lumberjacks.

15
00:01:08,001 --> 00:01:09,000
Keep your feet.

16
00:01:09,001 --> 00:01:10,000
Get in.

17
00:01:10,001 --> 00:01:13,000
From the pros.

18
00:01:13,001 --> 00:01:16,000
Three, two, one, go.

19
00:01:16,001 --> 00:01:18,000
To the college amateurs.

20
00:01:18,001 --> 00:01:19,000
Long shots.

21
00:01:19,001 --> 00:01:20,000
Long shots.

22
00:01:20,001 --> 00:01:21,000
You're looking for a game.

23
00:01:21,001 --> 00:01:23,000
Keep it up here.

24
00:01:23,001 --> 00:01:26,100
The woodsman team from Paul Smith's College in upstate New

25
00:01:26,200 --> 00:01:27,700
York ranks among the best.

26
00:01:37,240 --> 00:01:41,760
Their razor sharp axes make quick work of solid wood,

27
00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:44,260
or anything else that gets in the way.

28
00:01:44,360 --> 00:01:46,160
It looks dangerous because it is.

29
00:01:46,260 --> 00:01:48,800
These are six pound razor blades that you're swinging

30
00:01:48,900 --> 00:01:50,160
right between your legs.

31
00:01:50,260 --> 00:01:51,500
More than one guy has learned the nickname

32
00:01:51,501 --> 00:01:53,700
named Stumpy in this sport.

33
00:01:53,800 --> 00:01:56,700
I was at a competition one time and a guy took his

34
00:01:56,800 --> 00:01:59,540
big toe off and we spent the next half hour all of the

35
00:01:59,640 --> 00:02:01,480
competitors crawling through the grass looking for his

36
00:02:01,580 --> 00:02:03,580
toe.

37
00:02:03,680 --> 00:02:05,840
Some measure of safety may be provided by a chain

38
00:02:05,940 --> 00:02:11,260
mail sock to prevent an axe from cutting flesh.

39
00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:14,760
This is actually a butcher's chain mail down here.

40
00:02:14,860 --> 00:02:17,860
This will stop an axe but it's not going to stop it

41
00:02:17,960 --> 00:02:19,220
from breaking a bone.

42
00:02:19,221 --> 00:02:21,220
This is your cadence.

43
00:02:21,221 --> 00:02:24,220
Three, two, one, go!

44
00:02:24,221 --> 00:02:28,220
Timber sports came straight from 19th century logging camps,

45
00:02:28,221 --> 00:02:30,220
where the outcome of a bet for a pint of whiskey

46
00:02:30,221 --> 00:02:32,220
or the duty of cleaning the outhouse

47
00:02:32,221 --> 00:02:35,220
balanced on the lumberjack's prowess with an axe.

48
00:02:37,220 --> 00:02:40,220
Serious competitors swing a serious axe,

49
00:02:40,221 --> 00:02:44,220
a highly specialized racing axe.

50
00:02:44,221 --> 00:02:48,220
While an axe at the local hardware store costs about 30 bucks,

51
00:02:48,221 --> 00:02:53,220
A racing axe like this can set you back up to $500.

52
00:02:53,221 --> 00:02:55,220
If you were to go and take a hardware store axe

53
00:02:55,221 --> 00:02:57,220
and come out here and try to chop this,

54
00:02:57,221 --> 00:02:58,220
we'd still be chopping right now,

55
00:02:58,221 --> 00:03:01,220
and we'd probably be chopping into the night.

56
00:03:01,221 --> 00:03:04,220
This is the Ferrari of the axe world.

57
00:03:04,221 --> 00:03:06,220
Forged from high-quality steel,

58
00:03:06,221 --> 00:03:09,220
the head is thinner than that of a traditional axe,

59
00:03:09,221 --> 00:03:11,220
with a longer 7 1⁄2-inch blade

60
00:03:11,221 --> 00:03:14,220
that's sharp enough for a close shave.

61
00:03:14,221 --> 00:03:17,220
It'll take your hair right off.

62
00:03:17,221 --> 00:03:22,460
That'll go right through a toe or a finger.

63
00:03:22,560 --> 00:03:24,460
The finely tuned racing axe operates

64
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:27,160
according to the same laws of physics as every axe.

65
00:03:29,960 --> 00:03:34,680
An axe is a classic example of the simple machine, the wedge.

66
00:03:34,780 --> 00:03:38,180
The head is a wedge with a thin, sharp point of contact,

67
00:03:38,280 --> 00:03:40,080
the cutting edge.

68
00:03:40,180 --> 00:03:43,780
The sharper the edge, the more efficient the axe.

69
00:03:43,880 --> 00:03:45,980
The racing axe epitomizes this.

70
00:03:45,981 --> 00:03:50,780
They're meant to cut green wood as opposed to dry wood so that

71
00:03:50,880 --> 00:03:55,220
the very wet wood fibers actually explode as the blade comes

72
00:03:55,320 --> 00:03:57,620
into contact with it.

73
00:03:57,720 --> 00:04:00,220
An axe head by itself is useful,

74
00:04:00,320 --> 00:04:02,700
but add another simple machine, a lever,

75
00:04:02,800 --> 00:04:05,100
in the form of a handle or a haft,

76
00:04:05,200 --> 00:04:08,100
and the power of the axe increases.

77
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:13,100
The axe head may be the star, but the haft delivers the power.

78
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:15,960
The handle is a very important component of the

79
00:04:15,980 --> 00:04:18,980
because it amplifies the power coming from the chopper

80
00:04:19,080 --> 00:04:21,980
to the ax head and ultimately to the block of wood.

81
00:04:24,680 --> 00:04:28,120
Dissecting the parts of an ax sounds like an anatomy lesson.

82
00:04:28,220 --> 00:04:36,840
Throat, belly, shoulder, butt, eye, cheek, beard, heel,

83
00:04:36,940 --> 00:04:38,740
and toe.

84
00:04:38,840 --> 00:04:40,740
Some people have surmised that maybe the reason

85
00:04:40,840 --> 00:04:43,340
that the toe is called the toe is because that's the part

86
00:04:43,341 --> 00:04:48,080
That's the part that it most often removes from you.

87
00:04:48,081 --> 00:04:59,120
Some woodsmen study biomechanics to deliver optimal power with every stroke.

88
00:04:59,121 --> 00:05:04,180
They found that taking the ax behind the head is wasted motion.

89
00:05:04,181 --> 00:05:09,340
Now if you bring it straight down, that's good and all, but you can create more power,

90
00:05:09,341 --> 00:05:13,340
a greater biomechanical advantage, that is, if you throw the axe out.

91
00:05:13,341 --> 00:05:18,340
The most efficient chopper delivers a rolling blow.

92
00:05:18,341 --> 00:05:24,340
That's the whole reason that we don't present the whole bit of the axe into the wood, but

93
00:05:24,341 --> 00:05:30,340
instead, first the heel, followed by the belly and then the toe.

94
00:05:30,341 --> 00:05:37,340
The underhand chop echoes the moves of a lumberjack bucking a fallen tree, while the standing

95
00:05:37,341 --> 00:05:42,240
resembles a lumberjack chopping down a tree.

96
00:05:42,340 --> 00:05:45,240
But axe throwing? This challenge is pure sport.

97
00:05:52,280 --> 00:05:54,500
To double your chances of sticking the blade,

98
00:05:54,600 --> 00:05:57,400
a throwing axe has two sharp edges called bits.

99
00:06:00,400 --> 00:06:03,400
The lumberjack throws the three-pound axe overhand,

100
00:06:03,500 --> 00:06:06,400
20 feet to the target. Hitting the four-inch bullseye

101
00:06:06,401 --> 00:06:11,700
takes a well-balanced axe, a sharp edge, and a keen eye.

102
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:14,300
There it is, bullseye.

103
00:06:14,400 --> 00:06:17,040
The same goes for the lumberjills.

104
00:06:17,140 --> 00:06:19,140
Her thumb's pointed straight up to keep the axe

105
00:06:19,240 --> 00:06:20,640
from over-rotating.

106
00:06:20,740 --> 00:06:22,540
Everything's in a perfect line.

107
00:06:22,640 --> 00:06:23,860
And there it is.

108
00:06:23,960 --> 00:06:26,160
It makes you feel kind of powerful when

109
00:06:26,260 --> 00:06:27,560
you're holding an axe.

110
00:06:27,660 --> 00:06:30,960
You feel pretty darn tough, I'm not going to lie.

111
00:06:31,060 --> 00:06:35,760
Racing or not, modern axes are mass produced out of steel.

112
00:06:35,761 --> 00:06:41,000
But how do you turn six pounds of this into one of these?

113
00:06:41,100 --> 00:06:43,200
In Lake Waccamaw, North Carolina,

114
00:06:43,300 --> 00:06:46,500
ax making is a family operation.

115
00:06:46,600 --> 00:06:49,900
In business since 1886, Council Tool

116
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:52,520
is one of the few remaining ax makers in the United States

117
00:06:52,620 --> 00:06:54,620
today.

118
00:06:54,720 --> 00:06:57,820
The company makes about two dozen different axes,

119
00:06:57,920 --> 00:07:00,120
but they each start with 20-foot bars of steel.

120
00:07:02,720 --> 00:07:04,260
Using hydraulic machinery, they can

121
00:07:04,261 --> 00:07:06,920
that can exert 80 tons of force.

122
00:07:06,921 --> 00:07:09,460
Workers shear the bars into six pound chunks.

123
00:07:15,380 --> 00:07:18,000
You can see where the steel moved just a little bit,

124
00:07:18,001 --> 00:07:19,920
that's maybe an eighth of an inch,

125
00:07:19,921 --> 00:07:21,520
and then it broke off.

126
00:07:22,580 --> 00:07:24,380
Shaping the ax is a process as old

127
00:07:24,381 --> 00:07:25,680
as blacksmithing itself.

128
00:07:26,980 --> 00:07:28,080
Heat it and beat it.

129
00:07:30,480 --> 00:07:33,380
An induction coil furnace uses a magnetic field

130
00:07:33,381 --> 00:07:39,280
to heat the steel pieces to more than 2,000 degrees Fahrenheit.

131
00:07:39,281 --> 00:07:42,680
With a solid grip and some serious hearing protection,

132
00:07:42,681 --> 00:07:45,340
the hammer operator works the hot steel in a die

133
00:07:45,341 --> 00:07:48,880
under the 3,500 pound hammer.

134
00:07:48,881 --> 00:07:51,580
About 10 blows shapes the steel into an ax head.

135
00:07:56,600 --> 00:07:58,700
After trimming, the still red hot ax head

136
00:07:58,701 --> 00:08:02,780
undergoes another brute force operation.

137
00:08:02,781 --> 00:08:06,140
A machine called an upsetter grips the head

138
00:08:06,141 --> 00:08:09,320
and with 280 tons of force punches out the eye

139
00:08:09,321 --> 00:08:12,280
where the handle will later be attached.

140
00:08:12,281 --> 00:08:13,820
It's actually harder for the machine

141
00:08:13,821 --> 00:08:17,080
to pull the punch back out than it is to push it in

142
00:08:17,081 --> 00:08:21,040
because the eye is trying to cool and grab it.

143
00:08:21,041 --> 00:08:23,680
Once the axe head cools, craftsmen in the finishing room

144
00:08:23,681 --> 00:08:25,520
use belt grinders to clean its shape.

145
00:08:28,640 --> 00:08:32,740
Then comes the crucial moment when the bit is hardened.

146
00:08:32,741 --> 00:08:36,180
A small induction coil heats the bit of the axe to a little over

147
00:08:36,280 --> 00:08:39,180
1,500 degrees.

148
00:08:39,280 --> 00:08:42,080
Then a water-based solution quenches the axe,

149
00:08:42,180 --> 00:08:46,680
quickly cooling it to lock hardness into only the blade.

150
00:08:46,780 --> 00:08:52,000
If the entire head was hardened, the thin eye section might break.

151
00:08:52,100 --> 00:08:54,900
Next, in another shower of sparks,

152
00:08:55,000 --> 00:09:00,500
a grinder operator sharpens the bit to exact specifications.

153
00:09:00,501 --> 00:09:04,300
Then, workers dip the axe head in water-soluble enamel,

154
00:09:04,400 --> 00:09:07,300
but will later be baked for a durable finish.

155
00:09:07,400 --> 00:09:13,740
You can order any color you want, so long as it's black or red.

156
00:09:13,840 --> 00:09:17,660
Finally, the axe head is ready for the haft.

157
00:09:17,760 --> 00:09:19,960
This is a very critical step.

158
00:09:20,060 --> 00:09:23,560
We're joining a Hickley handle to the tool head itself.

159
00:09:23,660 --> 00:09:24,860
The handle is dried.

160
00:09:24,960 --> 00:09:27,800
We keep them in a humidity-controlled room

161
00:09:27,801 --> 00:09:32,740
room so that the material doesn't shrink and become loose.

162
00:09:32,741 --> 00:09:36,700
A hydraulic press fixes the head to the wooden handle.

163
00:09:36,701 --> 00:09:39,520
The same press pushes in a serrated aluminum wedge

164
00:09:39,521 --> 00:09:43,420
to lock the joint solid.

165
00:09:43,421 --> 00:09:45,220
How solid?

166
00:09:45,221 --> 00:09:46,880
Random samples are put to the test.

167
00:09:49,620 --> 00:09:52,320
This machine represents the blow that would be delivered

168
00:09:52,321 --> 00:09:56,560
by an average built 180-pound man.

169
00:09:56,561 --> 00:09:58,900
The contraption induces shock into the head

170
00:09:58,901 --> 00:10:02,600
to simulate typical use.

171
00:10:02,601 --> 00:10:06,840
But that's just the warm-up for the so-called pull test.

172
00:10:06,841 --> 00:10:09,840
To test how tightly the handle is joined to the head,

173
00:10:09,841 --> 00:10:13,680
thousands of pounds of pressure pull on the handle.

174
00:10:13,681 --> 00:10:17,280
A passing grade is 1,000 pounds.

175
00:10:17,281 --> 00:10:20,460
This axe survived 6,000 pounds of force

176
00:10:20,461 --> 00:10:24,620
before the handle began to pull out of the head.

177
00:10:24,621 --> 00:10:30,920
tool ships 10,000 to 20,000 axes every month, a far cry from the output of mankind's earliest

178
00:10:30,921 --> 00:10:38,680
axe makers in the Paleolithic age, who relied only on a stone, a piece of flint, and a steady

179
00:10:38,681 --> 00:10:39,381
hand.

180
00:10:39,382 --> 00:10:46,660
And the amazing thing about these tools is they lasted in the human inventory for more than

181
00:10:46,661 --> 00:10:48,042
a million years.

182
00:10:48,043 --> 00:10:54,520
The hand axe was primitive man's meal ticket to new sources of food. Now we

183
00:10:54,521 --> 00:10:56,520
and carry a leg off and eat and pee somewhere

184
00:10:56,521 --> 00:10:59,520
and not have to worry about something coming back to get us.

185
00:10:59,521 --> 00:11:01,520
And we could get into these bones,

186
00:11:01,521 --> 00:11:03,520
because in here, of course, is the marrow.

187
00:11:03,521 --> 00:11:05,520
Lots of nutrition, lots of calories,

188
00:11:05,521 --> 00:11:07,520
lots of fat, lots of protein.

189
00:11:09,520 --> 00:11:11,520
And there's the marrow right there.

190
00:11:11,521 --> 00:11:14,520
This is the first food processor.

191
00:11:14,521 --> 00:11:17,520
At some point in the later part of the Stone Age,

192
00:11:17,521 --> 00:11:20,520
the biggest development in Axis was invented,

193
00:11:20,521 --> 00:11:22,520
the first handle.

194
00:11:22,521 --> 00:11:25,220
This is a very important point.

195
00:11:25,320 --> 00:11:28,920
When you put a handle on an axe, then you up the ante.

196
00:11:29,020 --> 00:11:32,160
And you increase the efficiency, you increase the power,

197
00:11:32,260 --> 00:11:36,760
and you turn a very important tool into a super tool.

198
00:11:36,860 --> 00:11:39,100
Historian Steve Watts speculates

199
00:11:39,200 --> 00:11:42,640
that the first haft may have been made from flexible branches,

200
00:11:42,740 --> 00:11:47,720
such as willow, split, and bent over the stone axe head.

201
00:11:47,820 --> 00:11:50,640
So even as flimsy as that is,

202
00:11:50,641 --> 00:11:53,140
It's more efficient than holding it in my hand.

203
00:11:55,640 --> 00:11:58,640
Bundled with more branches and wrapped with rawhide,

204
00:11:58,740 --> 00:12:01,280
the hand axe transforms into a chopper.

205
00:12:05,580 --> 00:12:08,300
That's a super tool.

206
00:12:08,400 --> 00:12:11,360
Some 10,000 years ago, early man made a haft

207
00:12:11,460 --> 00:12:13,060
with a hole to accept the axe head.

208
00:12:16,460 --> 00:12:19,460
Here we're seeing the efficiency of this haft.

209
00:12:19,461 --> 00:12:22,760
We said before, it's not getting looser, it's getting tighter,

210
00:12:22,860 --> 00:12:27,760
because every swing of the axe tightens the head.

211
00:12:27,860 --> 00:12:31,200
With this tool, mechanically clearing land for agriculture

212
00:12:31,300 --> 00:12:32,400
first became possible.

213
00:12:34,900 --> 00:12:38,220
This is the stone axe at its height.

214
00:12:38,320 --> 00:12:42,320
5,000 years later, stone axes gave way to copper.

215
00:12:42,420 --> 00:12:47,360
1,000 years after that, copper gave way to bronze.

216
00:12:47,361 --> 00:12:50,460
Another 1,000 years passed before this bronze axe,

217
00:12:50,560 --> 00:12:53,260
crafted in the Middle East, featured new hafting

218
00:12:53,360 --> 00:12:56,100
technology, an eye.

219
00:12:56,200 --> 00:12:58,600
This means that the haft is going

220
00:12:58,700 --> 00:13:01,100
to be much less subject to splitting

221
00:13:01,200 --> 00:13:04,120
at the moment of impact.

222
00:13:04,220 --> 00:13:07,020
300 years later, around 1,000 AD,

223
00:13:07,120 --> 00:13:09,520
the axe was in the Iron Age.

224
00:13:09,620 --> 00:13:13,720
This is an axe that really means business.

225
00:13:13,820 --> 00:13:15,420
Throughout history, blacksmiths

226
00:13:15,421 --> 00:13:21,020
blacksmiths have applied heat and hammer to shape iron into an axe.

227
00:13:21,021 --> 00:13:24,360
They knew that some of the metal they worked was harder.

228
00:13:24,361 --> 00:13:27,860
They didn't know that this prized metal was actually an alloy of iron

229
00:13:27,861 --> 00:13:32,980
and carbon, steel. Even as frontiersmen explored

230
00:13:32,981 --> 00:13:37,880
North America in the 17th century, steel was in short supply.

231
00:13:37,881 --> 00:13:40,380
So blacksmiths saved it for the part of the axe that needed

232
00:13:40,381 --> 00:13:43,980
hardness the most, the cutting edge.

233
00:13:43,981 --> 00:13:47,080
Some places on the frontier, steel was certainly

234
00:13:47,180 --> 00:13:48,680
as valuable as gold is.

235
00:13:48,780 --> 00:13:51,480
You need a steel axe to cut trees down.

236
00:13:51,580 --> 00:13:54,720
A gold one won't do you much good.

237
00:13:54,820 --> 00:13:56,320
To make the most of the rare metal,

238
00:13:56,420 --> 00:13:59,520
blacksmiths developed the skill to fold an iron billet

239
00:13:59,620 --> 00:14:04,540
and forge weld a piece of steel in place for the bit.

240
00:14:04,640 --> 00:14:06,840
With an assist from some modern tools,

241
00:14:06,940 --> 00:14:10,380
blacksmith Paul Thorne creates this axe the same way a frontier

242
00:14:10,480 --> 00:14:11,280
blacksmith did.

243
00:14:13,980 --> 00:14:17,180
In a ballet of heating, hammering, and reheating,

244
00:14:17,280 --> 00:14:18,420
he adds the steel bit.

245
00:14:22,980 --> 00:14:26,020
Then shapes the pole, the back of the head.

246
00:14:28,920 --> 00:14:33,440
Next, he sizes the eye to accept the haft.

247
00:14:33,540 --> 00:14:35,340
Then a little grinding cleans up the ax.

248
00:14:37,940 --> 00:14:40,040
And he heat treats the steel bit.

249
00:14:40,140 --> 00:14:41,640
We're ready to quench that edge.

250
00:14:43,980 --> 00:14:48,420
Once cooled and polished, the axe is ready to be hafted.

251
00:14:48,520 --> 00:14:52,120
And then this axe would be used probably for a lifetime.

252
00:14:55,120 --> 00:14:56,620
The axe has a different identity

253
00:14:56,720 --> 00:14:58,720
beyond that of a versatile tool.

254
00:14:58,820 --> 00:15:02,940
It also became an intimidating and deadly weapon.

255
