But if one axe was good, then 1,000 axes

509
00:28:33,440 --> 00:28:35,140
must be great for this man.

510
00:28:40,440 --> 00:28:42,540
Larry MacPhail of Blaine, Washington,

511
00:28:42,640 --> 00:28:46,860
is one of many people who still heat their homes with wood.

512
00:28:46,960 --> 00:28:50,860
To keep the home fires burning, he splits his own.

513
00:28:50,960 --> 00:28:54,060
In the past 30 years, Larry has split almost 400 cords

514
00:28:54,160 --> 00:28:55,960
of wood.

515
00:28:56,060 --> 00:28:58,100
His biggest challenge isn't drumming up the energy

516
00:28:58,101 --> 00:29:00,240
for the task.

517
00:29:00,340 --> 00:29:02,840
It's choosing which axe to use from his collection

518
00:29:02,940 --> 00:29:05,840
of more than 1,000.

519
00:29:05,940 --> 00:29:08,440
This is just the beginning of the axe collection.

520
00:29:08,540 --> 00:29:10,140
I have another room that has some more.

521
00:29:12,640 --> 00:29:15,760
Larry's axe collection fills two garages.

522
00:29:15,860 --> 00:29:19,260
The lion's share of these that I've collected

523
00:29:19,360 --> 00:29:22,500
were actually used by loggers.

524
00:29:22,600 --> 00:29:24,260
For more than a century, the axe

525
00:29:24,360 --> 00:29:26,900
was among the prime movers powering the American logging

526
00:29:26,901 --> 00:29:29,300
the logging industry.

527
00:29:29,400 --> 00:29:32,400
Axe makers designed about 200 different patterns of tree

528
00:29:32,500 --> 00:29:37,140
felling axes, many named for the region they were made for.

529
00:29:37,240 --> 00:29:40,240
Notice how this axe is shaped the same on the top and the

530
00:29:40,340 --> 00:29:41,340
bottom?

531
00:29:41,440 --> 00:29:43,660
It's called a California reversible.

532
00:29:43,760 --> 00:29:46,560
If you turn it either way, it'd be exactly the same.

533
00:29:46,660 --> 00:29:47,860
This is a Michigan pattern.

534
00:29:47,960 --> 00:29:52,100
Notice how it's flat on the top and very swooping.

535
00:29:52,200 --> 00:29:56,500
And this was basically invented for the Michigan forest.

536
00:29:56,501 --> 00:30:00,000
19th century US ax makers created models like this out

537
00:30:00,100 --> 00:30:02,700
of sheer necessity.

538
00:30:02,800 --> 00:30:05,640
Axes imported from Europe lacked the balance and cutting power

539
00:30:05,740 --> 00:30:08,340
to bring down America's forests efficiently enough

540
00:30:08,440 --> 00:30:10,640
to meet the demands of the burgeoning logging industry.

541
00:30:13,520 --> 00:30:16,260
European axes typically had wide blades

542
00:30:16,360 --> 00:30:19,460
and very little mass behind the haft.

543
00:30:19,560 --> 00:30:23,100
Most of the weight was in front of the handle.

544
00:30:23,200 --> 00:30:25,300
North American axe designers built up the pole

545
00:30:25,301 --> 00:30:27,400
and narrowed the bit.

546
00:30:27,500 --> 00:30:31,240
The changes balanced the axe head over the handle,

547
00:30:31,340 --> 00:30:33,940
adding stability when striking a blow,

548
00:30:34,040 --> 00:30:36,740
focusing power on the cutting edge.

549
00:30:36,840 --> 00:30:39,720
You don't see it in any other countries in the world, really,

550
00:30:39,820 --> 00:30:43,080
that has the big pole head just like this and this long,

551
00:30:43,180 --> 00:30:45,720
slender, single bit.

552
00:30:45,820 --> 00:30:47,560
Another revolutionary design element

553
00:30:47,660 --> 00:30:51,720
appeared around 1860, the double bit.

554
00:30:51,820 --> 00:30:53,220
Why, too?

555
00:30:53,221 --> 00:30:56,760
To me, the most important part is they're perfectly balanced.

556
00:30:57,200 --> 00:31:00,500
And the other reason is they actually have two axes in one.

557
00:31:00,720 --> 00:31:04,320
When the one side gets dull, they can flip over and use the other side.

558
00:31:06,500 --> 00:31:09,140
An axe is still the favorite tool of these woodsmen,

559
00:31:09,860 --> 00:31:11,460
Frank Philbrick and his dad, Stephen.

560
00:31:12,500 --> 00:31:16,260
Their target today in western Massachusetts is a 60-foot pine.

561
00:31:17,780 --> 00:31:20,440
Even though a chainsaw may be faster and more efficient,

562
00:31:20,441 --> 00:31:23,780
They find satisfaction in doing it the old-fashioned way.

563
00:31:23,880 --> 00:31:28,040
The axe, definitely more reliable than the chainsaw,

564
00:31:28,140 --> 00:31:31,180
quieter, I would say probably safer,

565
00:31:31,280 --> 00:31:33,580
slower to get into trouble than a chainsaw.

566
00:31:33,680 --> 00:31:35,080
Someone could just go out with a saw

567
00:31:35,180 --> 00:31:38,300
and wind up in a tough spot instantly.

568
00:31:38,400 --> 00:31:41,400
At least you'd have to sweat first.

569
00:31:41,500 --> 00:31:43,300
And I see that that tree...

570
00:31:43,400 --> 00:31:45,800
Using a pendant as a plumb bob,

571
00:31:45,900 --> 00:31:48,200
Stephen checks the lean of the tree.

572
00:31:48,201 --> 00:31:50,000
They say felling is a negotiation

573
00:31:50,100 --> 00:31:52,000
between where you want the tree to fall

574
00:31:52,100 --> 00:31:53,500
and where it wants to land.

575
00:31:53,600 --> 00:31:54,500
10, 12 feet?

576
00:31:54,600 --> 00:31:55,400
Yeah.

577
00:31:55,500 --> 00:31:56,500
I don't know what's when we do.

578
00:31:56,600 --> 00:31:58,200
There's a certain element of mystery and doubt to this,

579
00:31:58,300 --> 00:32:01,740
which will probably make for good TV viewing, huh?

580
00:32:01,840 --> 00:32:02,640
I hope.

581
00:32:06,460 --> 00:32:07,760
That's it, huh?

582
00:32:07,860 --> 00:32:11,160
They plant a stake where they hope to bring the tree down.

583
00:32:11,260 --> 00:32:15,360
And Frank marks out where the first cut will be made.

584
00:32:15,460 --> 00:32:16,360
Bottom there.

585
00:32:18,200 --> 00:32:20,240
Top there.

586
00:32:20,340 --> 00:32:22,200
Using the same technique that lumberjacks

587
00:32:22,300 --> 00:32:24,740
used centuries ago, Frank and Steve

588
00:32:24,840 --> 00:32:26,740
will chop two carefully placed notches.

589
00:32:29,740 --> 00:32:32,240
The first notch, called the face cut,

590
00:32:32,340 --> 00:32:35,760
opens in the direction that they want the tree to fall.

591
00:32:35,860 --> 00:32:39,460
It will be chopped halfway through the trunk.

592
00:32:39,560 --> 00:32:43,500
This notch must have a 45-degree roof, a flat floor,

593
00:32:43,600 --> 00:32:46,160
and be large enough to avoid pinching the axe inside.

594
00:32:48,200 --> 00:32:49,960
On the opposite side of the tree,

595
00:32:50,060 --> 00:32:52,500
they'll cut the so-called felling notch,

596
00:32:52,600 --> 00:32:55,600
which must be higher than the first.

597
00:32:55,700 --> 00:32:58,740
If properly cut, the felling notch will leave a hinge

598
00:32:58,840 --> 00:33:01,340
that will help control and guide the tree as it falls.

599
00:33:03,540 --> 00:33:05,360
Nice.

600
00:33:05,460 --> 00:33:08,460
This little axe cut's nice.

601
00:33:08,560 --> 00:33:10,960
Frank wields his favorite double-bitted axe,

602
00:33:11,060 --> 00:33:13,100
made in Maine more than 75 years ago.

603
00:33:13,101 --> 00:33:15,100
There you go.

604
00:33:16,200 --> 00:33:21,340
These men who did this must have had absolutely

605
00:33:21,341 --> 00:33:23,340
prodigiously strong hands.

606
00:33:29,720 --> 00:33:31,560
How about our breather there?

607
00:33:31,561 --> 00:33:32,261
Yeah.

608
00:33:32,262 --> 00:33:33,660
You're doing huge work.

609
00:33:35,560 --> 00:33:37,120
And the breather's not going to be all that long

610
00:33:37,121 --> 00:33:38,400
before I'm worn out, so.

611
00:33:41,100 --> 00:33:42,460
There you go.

612
00:33:42,461 --> 00:33:45,460
There you go.

613
00:33:45,461 --> 00:33:48,200
Now you're putting the chips on the ground.

614
00:33:48,201 --> 00:33:51,200
Steve now switches to a modern store-bought axe,

615
00:33:51,201 --> 00:33:53,040
recently added to their collection,

616
00:33:53,041 --> 00:33:55,940
to see how the new compares to the old.

617
00:33:55,941 --> 00:33:56,641
The new one.

618
00:33:59,980 --> 00:34:01,580
Nah, see the way it's not?

619
00:34:01,581 --> 00:34:06,320
It doesn't hold its line on the downward strokes.

620
00:34:06,321 --> 00:34:08,420
It won't hold the angle he's hitting it.

621
00:34:08,421 --> 00:34:11,720
It does what's called scooping, and it's spitting it out.

622
00:34:11,721 --> 00:34:12,421
Yeah.

623
00:34:12,422 --> 00:34:14,420
OK, now try with that nice and sharp.

624
00:34:18,920 --> 00:34:21,000
After Frank completes the face cut,

625
00:34:21,001 --> 00:34:22,460
he takes a moment to sharpen his axe,

626
00:34:26,400 --> 00:34:30,180
and goes to work on the felling notch.

627
00:34:30,181 --> 00:34:32,540
If it's not cut right, the butt of the tree

628
00:34:32,541 --> 00:34:35,720
might shoot backward as it falls and injure or kill

629
00:34:35,721 --> 00:34:36,421
the woodsman.

630
00:34:36,422 --> 00:34:41,220
Three tons of tree will soon come crashing down.

631
00:34:41,320 --> 00:34:43,120
It's exciting.

632
00:34:43,220 --> 00:34:45,020
It's productive.

633
00:34:45,120 --> 00:34:47,060
It's dangerous.

634
00:34:47,160 --> 00:34:50,160
And there's just about a 10% of we just don't know.

635
00:34:57,280 --> 00:34:58,180
There we go.

636
00:34:58,280 --> 00:34:59,180
Timber!

637
00:34:59,280 --> 00:35:00,180
Timber!

638
00:35:00,280 --> 00:35:01,180
Yeah!

639
00:35:06,220 --> 00:35:07,220
That was great.

640
00:35:07,221 --> 00:35:08,220
That's satisfying.

641
00:35:08,221 --> 00:35:11,220
That's why everybody shouts timber.

642
00:35:11,221 --> 00:35:16,220
Honestly, that didn't take as long as I thought it was going to.

643
00:35:16,221 --> 00:35:19,220
How about that target?

644
00:35:19,221 --> 00:35:21,220
Not bad.

645
00:35:21,221 --> 00:35:22,220
Not bad at all, Lefty.

646
00:35:22,221 --> 00:35:23,220
Close enough.

647
00:35:23,221 --> 00:35:24,220
Yeah.

648
00:35:24,221 --> 00:35:25,220
Right there.

649
00:35:25,221 --> 00:35:26,220
Yeah.

650
00:35:26,221 --> 00:35:35,220
For centuries, axes not only brought down forests, but also shaped logs into timber for construction.

651
00:35:35,221 --> 00:35:39,220
In Monroe, Georgia, the Holder brothers, Witt and Gable,

652
00:35:39,221 --> 00:35:43,220
use RetroTek to make beams for building timber frame structures.

653
00:35:46,220 --> 00:35:49,220
This log is destined to replace a beam in a historical building,

654
00:35:49,221 --> 00:35:52,220
so the work requires historical accuracy.

655
00:35:54,220 --> 00:35:58,220
That means they rely on axes, and only axes,

656
00:35:58,221 --> 00:36:03,220
just like traditional hewing experts of centuries past.

657
00:36:03,221 --> 00:36:06,820
The brothers make the first cuts with felling axes.

658
00:36:06,920 --> 00:36:11,160
This is called juggling, this whole process.

659
00:36:11,260 --> 00:36:14,160
The sections in between the notches are called juggles.

660
00:36:14,260 --> 00:36:16,360
And the next stage will be to split those off.

661
00:36:16,460 --> 00:36:18,760
The whole purpose is so that we're

662
00:36:18,860 --> 00:36:21,580
removing a lot of wood at once, each of these juggles.

663
00:36:25,480 --> 00:36:28,980
They score the log.

664
00:36:29,080 --> 00:36:31,320
Then to cut a flat surface, they

665
00:36:31,321 --> 00:36:35,560
They use an axe with a bent head.

666
00:36:35,660 --> 00:36:40,560
This axe is a Swedish broad axe.

667
00:36:40,660 --> 00:36:45,760
The handle is straight, but the head is turned at an angle.

668
00:36:45,860 --> 00:36:48,480
The blade is parallel to the work,

669
00:36:48,580 --> 00:36:53,680
but my hands are safely away from the log.

670
00:36:53,780 --> 00:36:56,380
They use the broad axe to smooth the timber,

671
00:36:56,480 --> 00:36:59,620
leaving behind the distinctive pattern of hand-hewn wood

672
00:36:59,621 --> 00:37:03,320
that no modern techniques can duplicate.

673
00:37:03,420 --> 00:37:04,820
The Holder brothers based their work

674
00:37:04,920 --> 00:37:07,960
on their own forensic examination of old timbers

675
00:37:08,060 --> 00:37:12,200
to figure out exactly how logs were hewn hundreds of years ago.

676
00:37:12,300 --> 00:37:15,740
Think of it as hewing CSI.

677
00:37:15,840 --> 00:37:17,540
There's a lot of legends and myth,

678
00:37:17,640 --> 00:37:19,840
but the tool marks tell the tale.

679
00:37:19,940 --> 00:37:22,080
And when you can interpret them, then it's not guesswork,

680
00:37:22,180 --> 00:37:24,720
and we can actually know how they did it.

681
00:37:24,820 --> 00:37:26,520
The axe may have helped build homes

682
00:37:26,620 --> 00:37:28,820
and build lives on the frontier.

683
00:37:28,821 --> 00:37:32,220
But today, it's the tool for saving lives in an emergency.

684
00:37:39,460 --> 00:37:42,700
No axe may be more iconic than the one in the hands

685
00:37:42,800 --> 00:37:45,700
of a firefighter.

686
00:37:45,800 --> 00:37:48,440
Every firefighter who graduates from the Los Angeles Fire

687
00:37:48,540 --> 00:37:51,580
Training Academy carries one.

688
00:37:51,680 --> 00:37:54,140
If I get off the rig and I don't have it,

689
00:37:54,240 --> 00:37:55,740
I almost feel naked without it.

690
00:37:55,741 --> 00:37:58,340
So it's a part of me.

691
00:38:04,480 --> 00:38:06,880
The fire axe showcases its indispensability

692
00:38:06,980 --> 00:38:08,180
in this training exercise.

693
00:38:15,500 --> 00:38:22,840
It's tailor-made to break into burning buildings

694
00:38:22,841 --> 00:38:24,680
and cut ventilation holes in roofs.

695
00:38:28,580 --> 00:38:31,740
Even when firefighters have power tools at their disposal,

696
00:38:31,741 --> 00:38:35,980
they know that simpler can often be better.

697
00:38:35,981 --> 00:38:40,200
Chainsaws might stall, but this is always going to work.

698
00:38:40,201 --> 00:38:41,700
It doesn't require any gas.

699
00:38:41,701 --> 00:38:45,000
It doesn't require any extraordinary maintenance.

700
00:38:45,001 --> 00:38:49,100
And it's easy to use and user friendly.

701
00:38:49,101 --> 00:38:51,940
Most important of all, the firefighter's axe

702
00:38:51,941 --> 00:38:54,040
is a lifesaver.

703
00:38:54,140 --> 00:38:56,980
Many firefighters have rescued themselves and others

704
00:38:57,080 --> 00:39:00,840
by smashing through walls to create an escape route.

705
00:39:00,940 --> 00:39:02,880
I would have been dead.

706
00:39:02,980 --> 00:39:06,880
I'm here today because I had my ax with me.

707
00:39:06,980 --> 00:39:08,860
Captain Carlos Garcia's close call

708
00:39:08,960 --> 00:39:14,060
came in 1998 when he became trapped in a burning building.

709
00:39:14,160 --> 00:39:17,900
And fell back on basic training, pulled my ax off my side,

710
00:39:18,000 --> 00:39:20,900
and started going through the wall.

711
00:39:20,901 --> 00:39:24,140
Garcia finally succeeded, allowing other firefighters

712
00:39:24,240 --> 00:39:26,440
to locate and rescue him.

713
00:39:26,540 --> 00:39:28,100
Without that ax, I would have never

714
00:39:28,200 --> 00:39:29,640
got through that wall.

715
00:39:29,740 --> 00:39:32,240
Every single day, I strap this on when we go to a fire,

716
00:39:32,340 --> 00:39:36,140
and I make sure it's with me.

717
00:39:36,240 --> 00:39:39,160
The fire ax is the fusion of two tools.

718
00:39:39,260 --> 00:39:42,960
It features the spike of a pickeroon, a logging tool,

719
00:39:43,060 --> 00:39:44,560
and the blade of a flat-headed ax.

720
00:39:48,160 --> 00:39:49,860
The blade makes quick work of slicing

721
00:39:49,861 --> 00:39:53,360
through composite wood or thick roof planking.

722
00:39:56,260 --> 00:40:00,000
The spike is perfect for grabbing and ripping.

723
00:40:00,100 --> 00:40:02,900
It's also handy for gaining a foothold on a steep roof.

724
00:40:03,000 --> 00:40:05,200
Now he's got something to work off of.

725
00:40:05,300 --> 00:40:08,320
And it makes a life-saving emergency break.

726
00:40:08,420 --> 00:40:12,520
In case I go to step and I step and I slip,

727
00:40:12,620 --> 00:40:15,320
well, now I can hang off here.

728
00:40:15,420 --> 00:40:18,560
They can bring a ladder to me.

729
00:40:18,561 --> 00:40:21,260
Although axes can get firefighters out of a lot of trouble,

730
00:40:21,560 --> 00:40:22,800
they aren't indestructible.

731
00:40:24,100 --> 00:40:26,700
Their Achilles heel, the wood handle.

732
00:40:27,800 --> 00:40:29,940
It's the worst thing to have happen to a firefighter.

733
00:40:30,000 --> 00:40:31,540
They try and use their axe, and all of a sudden,

734
00:40:31,640 --> 00:40:34,480
they break the handle off the head, and they're useless.

735
00:40:34,780 --> 00:40:36,940
To be honest, I broke my last axe that was a wood handle.

736
00:40:37,040 --> 00:40:37,940
I've gone through three axes.

737
00:40:39,280 --> 00:40:41,040
Fiberglass handles offer the solution.

738
00:40:42,620 --> 00:40:45,720
The Nucla Company in Los Angeles makes fiberglass handles

739
00:40:45,721 --> 00:40:48,220
for one popular model of the firefighter's axe.

740
00:40:48,560 --> 00:40:53,560
Making a fiberglass handle begins with up to 150 bales

741
00:40:53,660 --> 00:40:55,300
of fiberglass.

742
00:40:55,400 --> 00:40:59,800
Each bale contains more than 100 strands.

743
00:40:59,900 --> 00:41:03,300
The strands undergo a process called ultrusion.

744
00:41:03,400 --> 00:41:08,220
It's pulled through the channels down toward the resin.

745
00:41:08,320 --> 00:41:11,820
The glass then proceeds into the dye.

746
00:41:11,920 --> 00:41:15,820
The dye compresses the glass.

747
00:41:15,821 --> 00:41:19,420
The dye heats the resin-coated strands to 300 degrees,

748
00:41:19,520 --> 00:41:22,460
speeding the curing process.

749
00:41:22,560 --> 00:41:24,160
Then the glass meets the tractor

750
00:41:24,260 --> 00:41:26,560
that puts the pull in poltrusion.

751
00:41:26,660 --> 00:41:29,360
And it'll travel 30 feet down to our cutter.

752
00:41:31,300 --> 00:41:34,480
And that's how we make a fiberglass handle.

753
00:41:34,580 --> 00:41:36,480
Nupla periodically tests handles

754
00:41:36,580 --> 00:41:40,140
in this screw-driven machine that can deliver 12,000 pounds

755
00:41:40,240 --> 00:41:42,620
of pulling pressure.

756
00:41:42,720 --> 00:41:45,820
First, a new hardware store acts for the glass

757
00:41:45,821 --> 00:41:55,820
with a wood handle. It splinters with 244 pounds

758
00:41:55,821 --> 00:42:01,440
of pull on the handle. This handle broke, and it

759
00:42:01,441 --> 00:42:03,920
leaves a very sharp piece at this end.

760
00:42:03,921 --> 00:42:09,460
And the tool end itself is also very dangerous to be around.

761
00:42:09,461 --> 00:42:11,320
Next, a fiberglass handle.

762
00:42:15,820 --> 00:42:20,820
The fiberglass handle gives way at 571 pounds,

763
00:42:20,920 --> 00:42:23,420
more than twice the load that destroyed the wood handle.

764
00:42:26,160 --> 00:42:27,660
The bottom line?

765
00:42:27,760 --> 00:42:31,160
Fire axes with fiberglass handles almost never break,

766
00:42:31,260 --> 00:42:35,780
lending the firefighters' best tool a potent new dimension.

767
00:42:35,880 --> 00:42:38,080
Count this among the many ways the axe,

768
00:42:38,180 --> 00:42:42,720
in all its incarnations, will always be cutting edge.

769
00:42:42,820 --> 00:42:45,800
Whether it's used as a tool in the hand

